      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coffeeville takes its name from John Coffee, a planter and military leader who lived from 1772 to 1833.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffeeville%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Once a bustling center of railroad shops, Coffeeville helped keep the region connected during the height of rail travel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffeeville%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 19th century, the town served as a relay station for the Simms line of stage coaches, a key link for travelers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.coffeevillescrapbook.com/early-history-of-coffeeville",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A minor Civil War engagement, the Battle of Coffeeville, unfolded near the town in December 1862.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coffeeville%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Coffeeville shares the county seat of Yalobusha County with the neighboring town of Water Valley.",
